---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadDataset } from "../lib/data.ts";
import { ogUrl } from "../lib/img.ts";
const ds = await loadDataset();
const ogFile = (ds.projects.find((p) => p.featured) ?? ds.projects[0])?.photos[0]?.file;
const name = ds.business.legalName;
---
<Base ogImage={ogFile ? ogUrl(ogFile) : undefined} title={`Privacy | ${ds.business.name.replace(" (sample)", "")}`} description="What personal information this site collects, why, who handles it, how long it is kept and how to ask for it or have it removed." canonical="/privacy/" business={ds.business}>
  <div class="section story">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Sample page, version privacy-2026-10-2. It is modelled on the BC Information and Privacy Commissioner's guidance for a privacy policy under the Personal Information Protection Act, and covers who is responsible, what is collected and why, consent, who handles it, how long it is kept, safeguards, access and corrections, and complaints. It is a starting point for a lawyer to review, not legal advice.</p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>{name} is responsible for the personal information it collects, including information handled for it by the services named below. Privacy officer: Office Manager, <a href={`mailto:${ds.business.email}`}>{ds.business.email}</a>.</p>

    <h2>What we collect and why</h2>
    <p>When you ask for a quote we collect your name, phone number, optionally your email and neighbourhood, and what you tell us about the roof. We use it to prepare your quote and to contact you about it. Each quote request is sent to the company through a notification service it sets up, such as an email relay or a team messaging channel (on this sample the form is not connected, so nothing typed into it is sent or stored).</p>
    <p>Only if you tick the separate advertising box on the form, we also keep the page you first landed on, the campaign details in the address you arrived by, the advertisement's click identifier and the time you clicked it (if you arrived from an advertisement), and a random Google Analytics visitor number from your browser, so we can tell which marketing works. If you leave that box unticked, none of those are sent with your request. Your browser does hold the campaign details and the page you first landed on in its session storage while you browse, and they are cleared when you close the tab. Separately, on every page load and before you choose anything, Google Analytics receives your internet address, device details, the page address (including any campaign details or advertisement click identifier in it) and a random visitor number from a cookie, a call-tap event is sent to it when you tap a phone number, and a lead event when a quote request goes through, whether or not you tick the box. The box controls only what we store with your request and whether your details go to Google Ads and Google Analytics afterwards (see Cookies below).</p>

    <h2>Consent</h2>
    <p>By sending the quote form you agree to us using what you typed to prepare your quote and to contact you about it. Nothing else is a condition of getting a quote. Two things are separate and each has its own box on the form, which is never ticked for you: marketing email, and the advertising measurement described above. You can withdraw either at any time by emailing the privacy officer, and marketing also with the unsubscribe link in each email. Withdrawing does not affect your quote. If you withdraw the advertising box we stop sending your phone number and email to Google Ads, we stop sending click details and your visitor number to Google Ads and Google Analytics, and we delete the click details, landing page and visitor number we stored with your request. A copy of the page address, without the campaign details, may remain in a notification we already received. Analytics page views continue as described under Cookies, and you can block them in your browser. What Google already received follows its own settings.</p>

    <h2>Phone calls</h2>
    <p>Once call tracking is connected (it is not on this sample), calls to the tracking numbers on this site are recorded, and a short announcement at the start of the call says so. We use the recording to prepare your estimate and to check the quality of our service. If you would rather not be recorded, use the contact form or call the number in the footer, which is not recorded. The tracking service also records your number, the source of the call and the page you were on, and we use that to measure our advertising; the announcement at the start of the call says that too, including that a scrambled copy of your number may be sent to Google.</p>

    <h2>Who handles it for us</h2>
    <p>Call tracking and call recording: CallRail. Website analytics and advertising measurement: Google (Analytics, Tag Manager and Ads). Hosting: Cloudflare. Content management, and delivery of the photographs and videos on this site to your browser: Sanity. Drafting of project descriptions from the crew's job notes and photographs: by default software running on the company's own computer, so nothing leaves the building. If the company chooses a hosted AI service instead, that provider receives the job notes and photographs the crew entered. Customer names, phone numbers, email addresses and street addresses are not part of what is sent, and the draft is checked for them before anyone reads it, but the crew's free-text notes are typed by people, so the crew is told not to put them there. Photographs are sent as taken with location data removed, and a photograph could show a house number, a face or a licence plate, so the crew chooses photos with that in mind. How long a hosted provider keeps those inputs, and whether it may use them for training, is set by that provider's terms, which the company must read and name here before choosing one.</p>

    <h2>Sharing for advertising measurement</h2>
    <p>If the company switches on conversion reporting (it is off on this sample), then once a quote turns into a booked estimate or a sold job, a one-way scrambled (hashed) copy of your phone number and email address, the click identifier and the job value can be sent to Google Ads, and the stage and value can be sent to Google Analytics, so the company can see which advertisements led to work. For a quote form that happens only if you ticked the advertising box, and the company's software refuses to build the upload when you did not. For a phone call it relies on the announcement at the start of the call. This site does not sell your information.</p>

    <h2>Cookies and advertising</h2>
    <p>This site uses cookies and similar technology for analytics and, if you arrive from an advertisement, for measuring it. Google Analytics and Tag Manager load as soon as a page opens, so their cookies are set before you choose anything; Google Analytics receives each page view directly from your browser, and Google's tag can contact other Google addresses as part of its normal operation, although this property's Google signals setting is off. How Google uses that information is explained at <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener">policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites</a>. You can turn off personalised advertising in your Google account's ad settings at any time, block cookies in your browser, or use Google's browser add-on to opt out of Analytics. If the company turns on Google's advertising features in Analytics, it will name them here and explain how first-party and third-party cookies are used together.</p>

    <h2>Marketing email</h2>
    <p>We only send promotional email if you tick the separate box on the quote form. Every email says who it is from, gives our mailing address and email address and has an unsubscribe link, and we stop without delay, and in any event within 10 business days of a request.</p>

    <h2>Photos and reviews</h2>
    <p>We publish photographs of finished work and show neighbourhoods, not street addresses. We only show a customer's name, photo or review with their written permission.</p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>Phone numbers, scrambled email addresses and the details of each quote request and call in the company's own records: 24 months, then deleted. Your name and message arrive as a notification, which the company keeps only as long as it needs to prepare and follow up your quote, then deletes. Call recordings, call logs and form submissions held by CallRail are kept by CallRail for 25 months, and its backups can hold deleted data for up to a further 90 days; CallRail may keep a caller's contact details for as long as the company's account is open, unless the company asks it to delete them. Information already sent to Google Analytics, Google Ads or a team messaging address is held under those services' own settings, not the 24 months. Analytics receives page views from every visitor, a phone-tap event from anyone who taps a phone number, and a lead event from anyone who sends the quote form, as described under Cookies; your details and the advertising details are sent to Google Ads or a messaging channel only as described above. The company remains responsible for what it sends.</p>

    <h2>Safeguards</h2>
    <p>The company limits access to this information to the people who need it, and chooses services that state how they protect it. No system is perfectly secure, and we will tell you if a breach is likely to harm you.</p>

    <h2>Your access and corrections</h2>
    <p>You can ask to see the information we hold about you, to correct it if it is wrong, or to ask for it to be deleted, by emailing the privacy officer above. We correct what is wrong and tell anyone we sent it to in the past year, we delete what we are not required to keep, and we answer within 30 days.</p>

    <h2>Complaints</h2>
    <p>If you are unhappy with how we handled your information, write to the privacy officer first. You can also complain to the Office of the Information and Privacy Commissioner for British Columbia (oipc.bc.ca).</p>
  </div>
</Base>
